From ab94810051d13310ea8f21d855e097ccfcb9bac9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cary Palmer <24235924+professorpalmer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:53:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cuda: sparse attention on by default for 8 GB, without a
 shallow-depth cost (late reserve + self-timing engage)

Late reserve (GGML_CUDA_KV_TIER_DEMOTE_FRAC=d): the top d of every VRAM head of
a tiered KV buffer is its own VMM allocation. Below the depth where sparse
attention engages, the dense line is kept. When it first engages (an attention
op's host tail reaches GGML_CUDA_FA_SPARSE_MIN_HOST), the engine syncs, copies
that chunk to pinned system RAM, maps it at the same address (with a VRAM
staging slot of its own), frees the VRAM, and the page bounds use it. The tier
line, staging, write-back and the CUDA-graph check move with it; kernel
addresses do not change. The host runs a range touches are handled as one span.

Self-timing engage (GGML_CUDA_FA_SPARSE_AUTO=1): per 16k band of context, the
first decode graphs past MIN_HOST time dense (dense line) and then sparse (after
the demotion); sparse is kept only when it is more than 3 % faster, else the
chunk moves back to VRAM. Decisions last for the process and are stored in
GGML_CUDA_FA_SPARSE_CALIB under GGML_CUDA_FA_SPARSE_CALIB_KEY, so a depth runs
the same mode on every request and after a restart.

RTX 4070, 2060-like line 80,640, q3_0: 64k unchanged (no demotion); the
decided state equals sparse off at 96k-245k (PCIe 4.0: dense wins every band);
a restart with the file loads the decisions with no trials and the same answer
bytes; a changed key times again; 245k needles equal to dense.
